A||N||6||Seanh||Need Help deciding which color to do my rims.||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||04-01-2001||03:56 PM||||I am refinishing my rims next week or two, and I still can't decided on what colors to use. My car is silver, with darker silver/gray? Ground FX. My current tires are all black, but my next ones are going to have white letters showing. I don't like the look of my rims now, with the darker gray in the middle and silver on the edges. I would like it all shiny silver. How would this look? Anyone have any pictures? How can I get it all shiny silver? Polishing, painting, what? Thanks<P>Also toss up any other ideas you think would look good on this car.||||reg||1
Z||000001||TwisT||04-01-2001||04:19 PM||||I still like all black centers on GT rims,  but have seen other colors that are impressive too.  Nice thing about black is that it will match any paint scheme.  <P>It all boils down to personal taste;  decide what over-all look you are going for the whole car first,  then the wheel color will come naturally.<P>Take a look around on the Internet for Fiero sites with pictures...  even this forum.  Someone is bound to have done what you are looking for.<P>Brett||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||04-01-2001||04:24 PM||||I'm kinda going for the chrome look with the rims. The reason I don't like them now is because from a distance they like a hole in the car. I don't like them the same color as the car, I just don't think it looks good. I would like it all shiny silver, without being chromed, but just polished and shined up. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Taijiguy||04-01-2001||05:19 PM||||I always like the look of dark centers with polished outer rims. I also like the center to match the trim colors, it pulls everything together. I think the shiney outer rim is an absolute must though.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Seanh||04-01-2001||05:23 PM||||The dark centers of my rims are in good condition, but the outside edges need to be polished. They have alot of marks and scrapes in them. How should I go about polishing them and making them shiny again? I was thinking just sand them down until I see no more marks and then polish them?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||04-02-2001||12:02 AM||||That's how it works; sand them down and then polish them.  It takes forever to do though.  The more I thought about it, the better sanding all the imperfections out with the wheel on the car sounds.  If you jack up the front, you could have someone spin the tire while you sit there with the sand paper and grind away.  It would work like a manually powered lathe.  Or, of course, you could jack the back up, start the car, set the car in top gear, put a brick on the gas pedal, and really make short work of the sanding process.  And who needs hands anyway?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||Raydar||04-02-2001||09:11 AM||||Eric, Sean, That's exactly how I did mine. <A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com/wheel.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com/wheel.htm</A> <BR>This link is on Darklord's Fiero project site.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
